{"id":"h108","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-chemistry","question":"When a solid melts, the entropy change and enthalpy changes expected are\nIs the correct answer \"negative entropy change and positive enthalpy change\"?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The proposed answer (negative entropy change, positive enthalpy change) is incorrect.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Melting is endothermic because heat is absorbed to overcome lattice forces, so ΔH > 0.","confidence":0.95,"lean":0.3,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"A liquid is less ordered than a solid and has more accessible microstates.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-0.8,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Melting increases entropy, so ΔS > 0.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"A negative entropy change would mean greater order, the opposite of what melting does.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-0.8,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"At the melting point ΔG = 0, so ΔS = ΔH/T_m, which is positive given ΔH > 0 and T_m > 0.","confidence":0.92,"lean":-0.9,"theta":1.4451,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The correct pairing is positive entropy change and positive enthalpy change.","confidence":0.96,"lean":-1,"theta":1.508,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The entropy sign is what decides whether the proposed answer is right.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-0.5,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":3,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":5,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":6,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":3,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":4,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":3,"j":4,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":3,"j"